---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
import { pomodoros } from '@/data/pomodoros';
---

<TimerPage
  title="Pomodoro Timer – Free 25/5 Focus Timer, No Login"
  description="Plan focus sessions with short breaks and a final long break. Adjust the Pomodoro timer, check the full cycle length and choose a task before starting."
  path="/pomodoro"
  h1="Pomodoro timer"
  lede="This Pomodoro timer gives you 25 minutes of focus, then a 5 minute break. After four rounds you get a long break, and then it stops."
  config={defaultConfigs.pomodoro}
  faq={faqs['/pomodoro']}
  related={relatedTimers['/pomodoro']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/pomodoro']}
  crumbName="Pomodoro timer"
>
  <Section title="What is the Pomodoro technique?" flush>
    <p>
      Pomodoro organises a chosen task into focus periods separated by breaks. A defined block can
      give you a manageable starting commitment; the pause gives you a point to review the next
      step.
    </p>
    <p>
      Try it for work you can protect from interruptions, such as writing, revision or a batch of
      administrative tasks. Adjust the length when the task needs a different stopping point. Our{' '}
      <a href="/blog/pomodoro-technique-25-5">Pomodoro introduction</a> explains planning,
      distractions and breaks before the timer settings.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Give the first block a clear task">
    <p>
      Choose something you can recognise as progress: draft a paragraph, check a set of figures or
      answer five questions. The familiar Pomodoro rhythm is 25 minutes of focus with short breaks,
      but the task inside the block matters too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Francesco Cirillo developed the{' '}
      <a href="https://www.pomodorotechnique.com/francesco-cirillo/">Pomodoro technique</a>. Our{' '}
      <a href="/blog/pomodoro-technique-25-5">practical guide</a> explains planning, interruptions
      and using a complete cycle.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Count the whole cycle">
    <p>
      The default contains four 25-minute focus blocks, three 5-minute short breaks and one
      15-minute long break. That is 100 minutes of focus and 130 minutes overall. The final long
      break replaces the last short break; it is not added after it.
    </p>
    <p>
      After the long break, the timer stops. Run again starts a fresh cycle. For one block, set
      Focus sessions to 1: that block is followed by the configured long break. For a single work
      period with no automatic break, use <a href="/interval">Interval</a> with Rest 0 and Rounds 1.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Choose a rhythm that fits">
    <p>
      Change Focus, Short break, Long break and Focus sessions before starting. The main page saves
      valid settings in this browser. Longer blocks can be convenient for a task with a slow setup,
      while a shorter commitment may help you begin.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no need to treat one setting as a universal optimum. Read the{' '}
      <a href="/blog/best-pomodoro-length">focus-length comparison</a>, or try a prepared rhythm
      below.
    </p>
    <ul class="links">
      {pomodoros.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <a href={p.path}>{p.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Use the cues as part of your plan">
    <p>
      The tab title shows the remaining time while the page runs. Sound cues can be delayed when a
      page is hidden or the device sleeps, so keep the timer visible when hearing each change
      matters. For a quiet pause rather than a work/break cycle, try{' '}
      <a href="/meditation">Meditation</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
